      <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-ink mb-2">1. Forward Osmosis Phenomenological Transport Equation</h3>
        <p class="mb-2">
          Unlike Reverse Osmosis (RO) which relies on severe external hydraulic overpressure (&Delta;P &gt; 60 bar), Forward Osmosis (FO) operates under near-ambient hydraulic pressure (&Delta;P &approx; 1&ndash;3 bar). Net water flux (Jw) is driven purely by the effective osmotic pressure gradient (&Delta;&pi;_eff) established across the thin active rejection layer:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-xl font-mono text-[11px] mb-2 text-ink">
          J_w = A &bull; [&pi;_(draw,m) - &pi;_(feed,m)] = A &bull; &Delta;&pi;_eff
        </div>
        <p class="text-[11px] text-muted">
          Where A is the pure water permeability coefficient (LMH/bar), and &pi;_(draw,m) and &pi;_(feed,m) represent the actual localized osmotic pressures directly at the active layer interfaces, heavily attenuated by concentration polarization.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-ink mb-2">2. Dilutive Internal Concentration Polarization (dICP) &amp; Structural Parameter S</h3>
        <p class="mb-2">
          When operating in FO mode (Active Layer facing Feed Solution, AL-FS), water permeating into the porous support layer dilutes the draw solution before it can exit into the bulk draw stream. This dilutive Internal Concentration Polarization (ICP) is the single most severe flux-limiting bottleneck in industrial FO:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-xl font-mono text-[11px] mb-2 text-ink">
          K = S / D_draw = (t_s &bull; &tau;) / (&epsilon; &bull; D_draw) &rArr; &pi;_(draw,m) = &pi;_(draw,bulk) &bull; exp(-J_w &bull; K)
        </div>
        <p class="text-[11px] text-muted">
          Where S = (t_s &bull; &tau;) / &epsilon; is the membrane structural parameter (&mu;m). Modern thin-film composite (TFC) and aquaporin membranes achieve S &lt; 250 &mu;m, boosting water flux by up to 350% compared to legacy cellulose triacetate (CTA) membranes (S &gt; 1,200 &mu;m).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-sm text-ink mb-2">3. Specific Reverse Draw Solute Flux (Js/Jw) &amp; Juice Purity</h3>
        <p class="mb-2">
          No membrane active layer is perfectly semipermeable. Draw solute molecules diffuse in the reverse direction—from the concentrated draw solution into the concentrated food product. The specific reverse solute flux ratio (Js / Jw) governs food-grade organoleptic and regulatory safety:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface border border-hairline rounded-xl font-mono text-[11px] mb-2 text-ink">
          J_s / J_w = B / (A &bull; R &bull; T) &le; 0.35 g/L
        </div>
        <p class="text-[11px] text-muted">
          For inorganic draws (NaCl, MgCl2), keeping Js/Jw &lt; 0.35 g/L ensures zero detectable salty or mineral off-notes in juices, meeting Codex STAN 247-2005 authenticity standards. Organic draws (sucrose, potassium citrate) have zero off-flavor risk.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl">
